APS-004 — Atlas Policy Language (APL)

Document ID: APS-004
Title: Atlas Policy Language (APL)
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines the Atlas Policy Language (APL), the canonical, declarative, implementation-independent language for expressing policies within the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

APL provides a standardized way to define policy intent, applicability, conditions, constraints, obligations, priorities, and effects while remaining independent of programming languages, rule engines, or execution technologies.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Policy language architecture
Language semantics
Expression model
Conditions
Constraints
Effects
Obligations
Composition
Extensibility

APL applies to every Canonical Policy Object.

3. Canonical Definition

The Atlas Policy Language (APL) is a declarative language for expressing policy semantics independently of execution mechanisms, enabling consistent interpretation, evaluation, and governance across all Atlas implementations.

APL expresses intent.

It does not prescribe implementation.

4. Objectives

APL SHALL:

Express policy semantics.
Remain deterministic.
Be implementation-independent.
Support machine interpretation.
Support human readability.
Enable interoperability.
Support extensibility.
Preserve explainability.
5. Language Principles

Every APL statement SHALL satisfy the following principles.

Declarative

APL SHALL describe what is required.

It SHALL NOT describe how to perform evaluation.

Semantic

APL SHALL express engineering meaning.

Implementation details SHALL remain external.

Deterministic

Equivalent policy inputs SHALL produce equivalent policy outcomes unless explicitly defined otherwise.

Readable

APL SHALL remain understandable by engineers and governance professionals.

Composable

Policy expressions SHALL be reusable and composable.

Versioned

Every APL definition SHALL reference a policy version.

6. Canonical Language Structure

Every APL policy SHALL define:

Element	Description
Policy Identity	Policy identifier
Applicability	Scope of application
Conditions	Evaluation criteria
Constraints	Required limitations
Effects	Policy outcome
Obligations	Required follow-up actions
Metadata	Governance information
7. Applicability Expressions

APL SHALL support applicability expressions for:

Object types
Engineering domains
Roles
Organizations
Projects
Geographic regions
Time periods
Lifecycle states
Trust levels

Applicability SHALL be explicit.

8. Condition Expressions

APL SHALL support logical conditions.

Canonical operators include:

Operator	Meaning
AND	All conditions must hold
OR	Any condition may hold
NOT	Negation
EXISTS	Presence check
FOR ALL	Universal quantification
FOR ANY	Existential quantification

Conditions SHALL remain side-effect free.

9. Constraint Expressions

Constraints MAY define:

Numeric limits
Cardinality
Temporal restrictions
Version requirements
Relationship requirements
Security requirements
Quality thresholds

Constraints SHALL be independently identifiable.

10. Effect Expressions

APL defines the following canonical effects.

Effect	Meaning
Permit	Allow action
Deny	Reject action
Require	Mandatory action
Recommend	Advisory guidance
Warn	Non-blocking notification
Escalate	Require higher authority

Every policy SHALL declare at least one effect.

11. Obligation Expressions

Policies MAY create obligations.

Each obligation SHALL specify:

Responsible actor
Required action
Completion condition
Deadline
Escalation rule

Obligations SHALL remain traceable.

12. Priority Model

Policies SHALL support priorities.

Example conceptual order:

Critical

↓

High

↓

Normal

↓

Low

↓

Informational

Priority SHALL influence evaluation order where applicable.

13. Composition

APL SHALL support policy composition.

Supported forms include:

Policy inclusion
Policy extension
Policy specialization
Policy aggregation
Policy inheritance

Composition SHALL preserve semantic independence.

14. Extensibility

Organizations MAY extend APL through:

Domain vocabularies
Custom object types
Engineering ontologies
Industry standards

Extensions SHALL preserve canonical language semantics.

15. Validation

Every APL definition SHALL be validated for:

Structural correctness
Semantic correctness
Determinism
Completeness
Consistency
Version compatibility

APL validation SHALL conform to ADS-015 — Validation Model.

16. Conformance

An Atlas Policy Language implementation conforms if it:

Supports canonical language elements.
Preserves declarative semantics.
Supports deterministic evaluation.
Supports policy composition.
Supports extensibility.
Preserves explainability.
17. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

Domain-specific language modules.
AI-assisted policy authoring.
Formal verification syntax.
International engineering vocabularies.
Semantic policy libraries.
Executable policy profiles.

Extensions SHALL preserve canonical APL semantics.

